[[File:Night in Taipei Grand Mosque 20130710.jpg|200px|thumb|left|Great Mashikura Mosque in [[Mashikura]]]]
Another religion based on local population is Islam which followed around 2% of Nakama population, mostly Fuuki People which already follow Islam before integration with Yamato People. About 10% of the Islam follower is actually foreign born migrants and their children while ethnic Yamato and Namiyan mostly convert upon marrying the imigrant or fuuki people. In 2016 estimated around 3 millions muslim in Nakama.

Christianity in Nakama introduced by the Jesuit mission starting from 16th century. However the spread of christianity was limited during early Kurahashi Shogunate, mostly on the trading port of Matsukawa and Shinko. Today around 2% of Nakamaese (2 millions) follow christianity. Despite of its low number of follower, Nakamaese adapted several cultures and custom originally related to Christianity into secular customs.

Other minority religions include Hinduism, Sikhism and Judaism, and since the mid-19th century numerous new religious movements have emerged in Nakama.

